    "# Supervised Machine Learning: Regression — Final Assignment Report\n",
    "\n",
    "--- \n",
    "## [1] Dataset Description\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.1 Overview, Source, and Structure of the Dataset\n",
    "- **Dataset Name & Source:** The dataset analyzed in this study is the **Clinical Diabetes Disease Progression Dataset**, originally compiled by **Efron, Hastie, Johnstone, and Tibshirani (2004)** (*\"Least Angle Regression,\" Annals of Statistics, 32(2), 407–499*) and distributed via `sklearn.datasets.load_diabetes`.\n",
    "- **Dataset Structure & Size:** The dataset comprises **442 clinical patient records (rows)** and **11 attributes (columns)**:\n",
    "  - **10 Baseline Predictor Variables ($X_1, \\dots, X_{10}$):** Demographic, physiological, and blood-serum measurements recorded at baseline.\n",
    "  - **1 Continuous Target Variable ($y$ — `Disease_Progression`):** A quantitative measure of diabetes disease progression recorded **one year after baseline** (ranging from `25.0` to `346.0`, with a mean of `152.13` and standard deviation of `77.09`).\n",
    "- **Data Quality & Completeness:** All 442 rows are complete clinical observations with **0 missing values (`NaN`)** and **0 duplicate records**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.2 Variable Roles, Data Types, and Clinical Descriptions\n",
    "| Variable Name | Original Code | Role | Data Type | Unit / Scale | Clinical Description & Distribution Summary |\n",
    "| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n",
    "| **`Age`** | `age` | Predictor ($X_1$) | Numerical (Continuous) | Years | Patient age at baseline (`Mean = 48.52`, `SD = 13.11`, `Range = 19.0–79.0`, nearly symmetric `Skew = -0.23`). |\n",
    "| **`Sex`** | `sex` | Predictor ($X_2$) | Categorical (Binary) | `1` or `2` | Biological sex (`1` = Female: 235 patients [53.2%], `2` = Male: 207 patients [46.8%]). |\n",
    "| **`BMI`** | `bmi` | Predictor ($X_3$) | Numerical (Continuous) | $\\text{kg/m}^2$ | Body Mass Index (`Mean = 26.38`, `SD = 4.42`, `Range = 18.0–42.2`, moderately right-skewed `Skew = +0.60`). Strongest univariate predictor ($r = +0.586$). |\n",
    "| **`Blood_Pressure`** | `bp` | Predictor ($X_4$) | Numerical (Continuous) | mm Hg | Average arterial blood pressure (`Mean = 94.65`, `SD = 13.83`, `Range = 62.0–133.0`, `Skew = +0.29`, $r = +0.441$). |\n",
    "| **`Total_Cholesterol`** | `s1` | Predictor ($X_5$) | Numerical (Continuous) | mg/dL | Total serum cholesterol (`Mean = 189.14`, `SD = 34.61`, `Range = 97.0–301.0`, `Skew = +0.38`, $r = +0.212$). |\n",
    "| **`LDL_Cholesterol`** | `s2` | Predictor ($X_6$) | Numerical (Continuous) | mg/dL | Low-density lipoproteins / \"bad\" cholesterol (`Mean = 115.44`, `SD = 30.41`, `Range = 41.6–242.4`, $r = +0.174$). |\n",
    "| **`HDL_Cholesterol`** | `s3` | Predictor ($X_7$) | Numerical (Continuous) | mg/dL | High-density lipoproteins / \"good\" protective cholesterol (`Mean = 49.79`, `SD = 12.93`, `Range = 22.0–99.0`, `Skew = +0.80`, $r = -0.395$). |\n",
    "| **`Total_HDL_Ratio`** | `s4` | Predictor ($X_8$) | Numerical (Continuous) | Ratio (`tc/hdl`) | Ratio of Total to HDL cholesterol (`Mean = 4.07`, `SD = 1.29`, `Range = 2.00–9.09`, `Skew = +0.73`, $r = +0.430$). |\n",
    "| **`Log_Triglycerides`** | `s5` | Predictor ($X_9$) | Numerical (Continuous) | $\\ln(\\text{mg/dL})$ | Natural log of serum triglycerides (`Mean = 4.64`, `SD = 0.52`, `Range = 3.26–6.11`, `Skew = +0.44`, $r = +0.566$). Second strongest predictor. |\n",
    "| **`Blood_Sugar`** | `s6` | Predictor ($X_{10}$) | Numerical (Continuous) | mg/dL | Fasting blood glucose concentration (`Mean = 91.26`, `SD = 11.50`, `Range = 58.0–124.0`, `Skew = +0.21`, $r = +0.382$). |\n",
    "| **`Disease_Progression`** | `target` | **Target ($y$)** | Numerical (Continuous) | Progression Index | Quantitative 1-year disease progression (`Mean = 152.13`, `SD = 77.09`, `Median = 140.50`, `IQR = 87.0–211.5`, `Skew = +0.44`). |"
   ]
