    "---\n",
    "## [2] Analysis Objective\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.1 Main Objective and What We Aim to Predict\n",
    "The primary objective of this regression analysis is to **predict a patient's quantitative 1-year diabetes progression score (`Disease_Progression`, continuous outcome $y \\in [25, 346]$)** using their 10 baseline physiological and blood-serum biomarkers, while simultaneously **identifying the most influential modifiable clinical risk factors** (`BMI`, `Log_Triglycerides`, `Blood_Pressure`, and `HDL_Cholesterol`) so healthcare providers can design targeted early interventions.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.2 Step-by-Step Analytical Plan\n",
    "1. **Exploratory & Collinearity Audit:** Inspect feature distributions, check pairwise Pearson correlations, and compute **Variance Inflation Factors (VIF)** to diagnose multicollinearity among the lipid panel variables (`Total_Cholesterol`, `LDL_Cholesterol`, `HDL_Cholesterol`, and `Total_HDL_Ratio`).\n",
    "2. **Data Partitioning & Standardization:** Split the 442 patient records into an **80% Training Set (353 patients)** and a **20% Held-Out Test Set (89 patients)** (`random_state=42`). Apply `StandardScaler` ($\mu=0, \sigma=1$) fitted strictly on the training set so regularized models penalize all features on an identical scale without data leakage.\n",
    "3. **Feature Engineering (Non-Linear Interactions):** Construct degree-2 polynomial and pairwise interaction features (`PolynomialFeatures(degree=2)`) to test whether synergistic metabolic interactions (such as simultaneously elevated `BMI` and `Log_Triglycerides`) improve predictive accuracy.\n",
    "4. **Model Training & Cross-Validation:** Train and compare **six Linear Regression variations** — Simple OLS (`BMI` only), Multiple OLS, Ridge Regression ($L_2$), Lasso Regression ($L_1$), ElasticNet ($L_1 + L_2$), and Polynomial Degree-2 Ridge — tuned via 5-Fold Cross-Validation.\n",
    "5. **Model Selection & Diagnostic Evaluation:** Compare models using held-out **Test $R^2$, Adjusted $R^2$, 5-Fold CV $R^2$, RMSE, and MAE**, interpret standardized coefficients, and inspect residual diagnostics.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.3 Key Statistical Hypotheses ($H_0$ vs. $H_1$)\n",
    "- **Hypothesis 1 (Adiposity & Lipid Drivers):**\n",
    "  - $H_0: \\beta_{\\text{BMI}} = \\beta_{\\text{Log\\_Triglycerides}} = 0$ (neither `BMI` nor triglycerides significantly predicts 1-year progression).\n",
    "  - $H_1: \\beta_{\\text{BMI}} > 0$ and $\\beta_{\\text{Log\\_Triglycerides}} > 0$ (`BMI` and `Log_Triglycerides` are the strongest positive linear predictors of disease progression at $p < 0.001$).\n",
    "- **Hypothesis 2 (Protective HDL Effect):**\n",
    "  - $H_1: \\beta_{\\text{HDL\\_Cholesterol}} < 0$ (higher protective HDL cholesterol significantly reduces 1-year disease progression).\n",
    "- **Hypothesis 3 (Regularization vs. Multicollinearity):**\n",
    "  - $H_1:$ $L_1/L_2$ regularized linear regression (`LassoCV`, `RidgeCV`, `ElasticNetCV`) will achieve higher out-of-sample Test $R^2$ and lower Test RMSE than unregularized Multiple OLS by shrinking collinear cholesterol noise.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.4 Anticipated Modelling Challenges\n",
    "1. **Severe Multicollinearity in the Lipid Panel:** Because $\\text{Total Cholesterol} \\approx \\text{HDL} + \\text{LDL} + 0.2 \\times \\text{Triglycerides}$, unregularized OLS suffers from extreme Variance Inflation Factors ($\\text{VIF} > 60$ for `Total_Cholesterol` and `LDL_Cholesterol`), leading to unstable sign-flipping coefficients.\n",
    "2. **Non-Linearity & Interaction Effects:** Purely additive linear models may underpredict patients who exhibit multiple concurrent metabolic risk factors.\n",
    "3. **Unobserved Clinical Confounders & Heteroscedasticity:** Lack of behavioral/genetic features (such as HbA1c, diet, and medication adherence) may cap explained variance near $R^2 \\approx 0.50$ and introduce mild heteroscedasticity."
   ]
