    "---\n",
    "## [3] Model Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3.1 Variations of Linear Regression Models Evaluated\n",
    "We trained, tuned via 5-Fold Cross-Validation, and compared **six distinct variations of Linear Regression**:\n",
    "1. **Model 1 — Simple Linear Regression (`BMI` Only):** Baseline single-feature Ordinary Least Squares model using the top univariate predictor (`BMI`).\n",
    "2. **Model 2 — Multiple Ordinary Least Squares (`LinearRegression` — All 10 Features):** Standard unregularized multivariable linear regression.\n",
    "3. **Model 3 — Ridge Regression ($L_2$ Regularization via `RidgeCV`):** Adds an $L_2$ penalty ($\\alpha \\sum_{j=1}^p \\beta_j^2$, optimal $\\alpha = 31.62$) to shrink correlated cholesterol coefficients and reduce variance.\n",
    "4. **Model 4 — Lasso Regression ($L_1$ Regularization via `LassoCV`):** Adds an $L_1$ penalty ($\\alpha \\sum_{j=1}^p |\\beta_j|$, optimal $\\alpha = 1.93$) to perform automatic feature selection by driving redundant collinear variables (`Total_Cholesterol`, `LDL_Cholesterol`, `Total_HDL_Ratio`, `Age`) to exact zero.\n",
    "5. **Model 5 — ElasticNet Regression ($L_1 + L_2$ Combined via `ElasticNetCV`):** Combines Lasso sparsity with Ridge grouping stability ($\\alpha = 0.52, l_1\\text{-ratio} = 0.70$).\n",
    "6. **Model 6 — Polynomial Degree-2 Ridge Regression:** Fits degree-2 quadratic and pairwise interaction terms regularized with strong $L_2$ penalty ($\\alpha = 359.38$) to capture non-linear metabolic synergy.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3.2 Justification of the Best Model for Our Objectives\n",
    "- **Best Model for Clinical Interpretability & Collinearity Resolution:** **Model 4 — Lasso Regression (`LassoCV`)** achieves a **Test $R^2 = 0.4669$** and **Test RMSE = `53.15`** (outperforming unregularized Multiple OLS `Test R² = 0.4526`, `RMSE = 53.85`) while reducing the model from 10 noisy predictors to **6 clean, non-collinear clinical features**.\n",
    "- **Best Model for Maximum Predictive Accuracy:** **Model 6 — Polynomial Degree-2 Ridge Regression** achieves the highest **Test $R^2 = 0.5042$** and lowest **Test RMSE = `51.25`** (`MAE = 40.85`) by capturing second-order biomarker interactions (`BMI × Log_Triglycerides`)."
   ]
  },
