    "---\n",
    "## [5] Evaluation, Limitations & Future Steps\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.1 Critical Evaluation & Potential Flaws in the Model\n",
    "No statistical model is perfect. Diagnostic inspection of our regression models highlights four specific limitations worth revisiting:\n",
    "1. **Omitted Variable Bias (~49% Unexplained Variance):**\n",
    "   - Even our best models (`Lasso` $R^2 = 0.4669$, `Polynomial Ridge` $R^2 = 0.5042$) leave roughly **49%–53% of outcome variance unexplained** (`Test MAE = 40.85–42.20` points). Crucial clinical and behavioral determinants of diabetes progression — such as **glycated hemoglobin (`HbA1c`), insulin/medication dosage and adherence, dietary intake, physical activity, diabetes duration, and genetic risk scores** — are absent from the dataset.\n",
    "2. **Mild Heteroscedasticity in Mid-to-High Predictions:**\n",
    "   - As shown in the *Residuals vs. Predicted Values* plot below, residual error spread is narrower at low predicted progression (`< 100`) and widens across the mid-to-high progression range (`140–230`), partially violating the Gauss-Markov homoscedasticity (constant error variance) assumption.\n",
    "3. **Cross-Sectional Single-Snapshot Predictors:**\n",
    "   - All 10 features were measured at a single baseline visit; the model cannot distinguish between a patient whose `BMI` is stable versus one experiencing rapid weight gain.\n",
    "4. **Sample Size & High-Order Polynomial Variance ($N = 442$):**\n",
    "   - Expanding to degree-2 polynomial features increases dimensionality from 10 to 65 features, which risks overfitting on $N=353$ training patients unless heavily regularized.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.2 Concrete Plan to Revisit and Improve the Analysis\n",
    "1. **Collect Additional Longitudinal & Clinical Data:**\n",
    "   - Augment the cohort with longitudinal **HbA1c trajectories, continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) time-in-range metrics, medication adherence logs, and lifestyle/dietary surveys** to capture the ~49% unexplained variance.\n",
    "2. **Apply Target Transformations & Generalized Linear Models (GLMs):**\n",
    "   - Apply a **Box-Cox or logarithmic transformation** ($\\ln(y)$) to `Disease_Progression` or fit a **Gamma GLM with a log link** to stabilize heteroscedastic residuals and guarantee strictly positive predictions.\n",
    "3. **Test Alternative Non-Linear & Probabilistic Predictive Methods:**\n",
    "   - Benchmark regularized linear regression against **Random Forests, Gradient Boosted Decision Trees (`XGBoost` / `LightGBM`), and Support Vector Regression (`SVR`)** coupled with **SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations)** to capture complex non-linear thresholds.\n",
    "   - Fit **Quantile Regression** (10th, 50th, and 90th percentiles) so clinicians receive calibrated **90% prediction intervals** alongside point predictions."
   ]
  },
